So the reason it's a macro is to avoid processing its String parameter if we're not actually printing verbose stuff:
```cpp
// This version avoids processing the text to be printed until it actually has to be printed, saving some CPU usage.
#define print_verbose(m_text)             \
	{                                     \
		if (is_print_verbose_enabled()) { \
			print_line(m_text);           \
		}                                 \
	}
```

> > So the reason it's a macro is to avoid processing its String parameter if we're not actually printing verbose stuff:
>
> An `inline` function would do the same, right?

I'm not sure. Tested with this script, it still executes the function, while the macro version doesn't.

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>

bool is_print_verbose_enabled() {
    return false;
}

#if 1
inline void print_verbose(std::string p_string) {
    if (is_print_verbose_enabled()) {
        std::cout << p_string << std::endl;
    }
}
#else
#define print_verbose(m_string) \
    if (is_print_verbose_enabled()) { \
        std::cout << m_string << std::endl; \
    }
#endif

std::string do_stuff_and_give_string() {
    std::cout << "Doing some stuff..." << std::endl;
    std::string ret = "Stuff was done!";
    return ret;
}

int main() {
    std::string hw = "Hello world";
    print_verbose(hw);
    print_verbose(do_stuff_and_give_string());
    return 0;
}
```
